{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Then Jacob was greatly afraid and it troubled him.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ויירא — lest he be killed; ויצר לו — if he kills others. And since the Holy One, blessed be He, had promised him (Genesis 28:15): ״והנה אנכי עמך ושמרתיך בכל אשר תלך״ — 'and behold, I am with you and will guard you wherever you go' — why was he afraid? Rather, lest sin cause it. Thus our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in the first chapter of Berakhot. And they further interpreted a similar matter: you ask why there is a dot over the ו of לולא — for thus said David (Psalms 27:13): ״לולא האמנתי לראות בטוב ה׳״ — 'had I not believed to see the goodness of YHWH.' 'Master of the universe, I am confident in You that You are destined to repay good reward to the righteous in the world to come, but I do not know whether I have a share among them or not.' And since he called himself a חסיד, how could he be in doubt about this? Rather, lest sin cause it.'" "<b>And he said.</b> Some interpreted that the reason Jacob was afraid was not for himself, for he had already been promised, but he was afraid for his sons and the people of his household, and this is what is written: <b>And he divided the children.</b> And he further said: <b>Lest he come and strike me, mother upon children.</b> He did not say 'and strike me' alone, for he did not fear for himself, but for the striking of mother upon children." "<b>And know</b> that this pericah contains an explanation of what befell Jacob with Esau his brother, and an allusion as well to the generations, to what is destined to befall us continually with the children of Esau. And it is fitting for us to grasp the ways of Jacob, who prepared himself for three things: for war, for prayer, and for a gift. <b>For war</b> — as it is written: ״ויחץ את העם אשר אתו״, and it is written: ״ויאמר אם יבא עשו״, etc. <b>For prayer</b> — as he said: ״אלהי אבי אברהם״, etc., ״הצילני נא״. <b>For a gift</b> — as it is written: ״עזים מאתים״, etc. And from him Hezekiah the king learned and did likewise, preparing himself for these three things against Sennacherib king of Assyria, as it is written (2 Kings 18:15): ״ויתן חזקיהו את כל הכסף״; and it is written (2 Chronicles 32:6): ״ויתן שרי מלחמות על העם״; and it is written (2 Kings 19:15): ״ויתפלל חזקיהו לפני ה׳״. And so we too must walk in the ways of the fathers, to prepare ourselves to encounter them with a gift and with soft speech and with prayer before HaShem, may He be exalted — but as for war, it is impossible, as it is said (Song of Songs 2:7): ״השבעתי אתכם בנות ירושלים״, etc. He adjured them not to provoke war with the nations."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:09.824307450Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}